# Cloudflare Pages headers — https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/
# Security headers carried over from the previous site so they survive the redesign.

/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()

# ─── Caching ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There is no build step here, so filenames carry no content hash and files are
# edited in place. `immutable` is therefore wrong for anything that changes: the
# first version of this file marked css and js immutable for a year, and the very
# next JS change could not be shipped — Cloudflare served the year-old copy from
# edge cache and browsers would have held it just as long.
#
# So only fonts are immutable, because a different font means a different family
# and therefore a different filename. Everything else revalidates. The cost is one
# conditional request per asset, answered with an empty 304, which is cheap.
#
# If you ever do want long-lived caching on css/js, add a content hash to the
# filename first. Do not simply raise max-age.

/*.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Re-read by crawlers, so never served stale for long.
/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600
/sitemap.xml
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# Images are replaced rarely but it does happen, so they revalidate after an hour
# rather than being locked in for a year.
/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

# Fonts genuinely never change in place — a new font is a new family and a new
# filename — so these are the one safe case for immutable.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The Intelligence film is scrubbed by scroll, so byte-range requests matter: the
# browser seeks into it rather than streaming from the start. It has already been
# re-cut several times, so it revalidates like the other media.
/*.mp4
  Content-Type: video/mp4
  Accept-Ranges: bytes
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate
